    title: "Merz vows to keep coalition together for Germany's 'democratic future'",
    summary: "The chancellor has come under increasing pressure after resounding state election defeats, but says the results \"go far beyond my own person\"",
    preview: "German Chancellor Friedrich Merz says he will keep his ruling coalition together for the sake of his country's \"democratic future\", following resounding defeats for his party in two state elections. \"We are determined to live up to the historic challenges our country is facing,\" Merz said, referring to the coalition between his Christian Democratic Unionist (CDU) party and the Social Democratic Party (SPD). Merz's position has come under mounting pressure after Sunday's election results.",
    body: "German Chancellor Friedrich Merz says he will keep his ruling coalition together for the sake of his country's \"democratic future\", following resounding defeats for his party in two state elections.\n\n\"We are determined to live up to the historic challenges our country is facing,\" Merz said, referring to the coalition between his Christian Democratic Unionist (CDU) party and the Social Democratic Party (SPD).\n\nMerz's position has come under mounting pressure after Sunday's election results. In the north-eastern Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, the far-right Alternative for Germany (AfD) party won the most votes, with the CDU failing to reach the threshold to even enter the state parliament.\n\nMeanwhile in Berlin, the CDU came second to the hard-left Die Linke party, with the AfD also making significant gains.\n\nSpeaking at the news conference on Monday, Merz described the result in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern as \"a heavy blow\".\n\n\"This election is a profound turning point. It is a dramatic, unprecedented result for the CDU in Germany,\" he said.\n\nHe however pushed back against the idea that his low popularity ratings were behind the election defeats.\n\n\"The reasons for these election results go far beyond my own person,\" he insisted.\n\nMerz is under mounting pressure after months of poor approval ratings, and there have been rumours he could be replaced mid-term in a so-called chancellor swap.\n\nAsked earlier on Monday how long Merz has left in office, the AfD's co-leader Alice Weidel told the BBC: \"Pretty soon, he's going to leave.\"\n\nThe two local elections took place on Sunday, a fortnight after the CDU saw its vote halved in the state of Saxony-Anhalt, where the AfD won a major victory.",
